\chapter{GNU, Linux, and the Work of Keeping Software Free}
\label{ch:linux}
\index{GNU}\index{Linux}\index{Stallman, Richard}\index{Torvalds, Linus}\index{free software}\index{open source}

\section{The operating system was larger than the kernel}

Richard Stallman announced the GNU project in 1983 and began its work in
1984. Its aim was a free Unix-compatible operating system: an environment
users could run, study, modify, and redistribute. Compatibility was a
strategic choice. Familiar interfaces made existing software and experience
useful, while a new implementation could establish different legal terms.
The recursive name ``GNU's Not Unix'' captured both the relationship and
the separation.

An operating system in everyday use needs far more than a kernel.
It needs a compiler and linker, libraries, a debugger, a shell, text tools,
an editor, installation conventions, and documentation. GNU's work on
Emacs, GCC, GDB, libraries, and utilities supplied many of these elements.
Their importance is obscured when the history is told as though a kernel
alone suddenly created a complete usable system \parencite[ch. 8, 10--12]{salus}.

The project also treated legal permission as part of the engineering task.
The Free Software Foundation, founded in 1985, supported this goal.
The GNU General Public License used copyright to impose conditions on
redistribution that preserved defined freedoms for recipients. It was not
a rejection of all rules; it was a carefully chosen set of rules intended
to prevent a familiar kind of loss of access.

GNU's intended kernel path eventually involved the Hurd, a collection of
servers built on a Mach foundation. The schedule and complexity of that
work mattered because other components were already useful. A system can
be incomplete as a project while contributing essential infrastructure to
other systems. That is precisely what made the later combination with
Linux possible.

\section{A kernel for an inexpensive machine}
\index{MINIX}

Linus Torvalds began Linux in 1991 while a student in Helsinki, working
with a 386-based personal computer. MINIX, Andrew Tanenbaum's teaching
system, was part of the surrounding context. Linux was a new kernel
implementation, not a rebranding of AT\&T Unix source. Its early ambition
was much smaller than the eventual range of machines and organizations
that would depend on it.

The distinction between inspiration and code descent matters. An interface
can be reproduced independently. A programmer can learn from a teaching
system without making the new kernel a derivative of that system's
implementation. Simplified family trees often blur these relationships by
using the same line to mean shared code, influence, or compatibility.

Linux's original licensing differed from the terms it later adopted.
The move to GPL version 2 in 1992 helped make the kernel fit the wider free
software ecosystem. Volunteers could combine it with existing tools and
share the resulting systems. GNU components were central to many of those
systems, though not every Linux-based environment would use the same
libraries or utilities.

This history explains the naming debate without resolving it by fiat.
``GNU/Linux'' emphasizes the construction of a complete system from Linux
and GNU components. ``Linux'' is the common shorter name in much everyday
usage. Android demonstrates why neither expression should be applied
mechanically: it uses the Linux kernel but a substantially different
userspace, including Bionic rather than the GNU C library.

\section{Distributions turn components into a system}
\index{distributions}\index{package management}

A kernel tarball, compiler, and collection of utilities are not yet an
installation experience. Someone must decide versions, build packages,
resolve dependencies, arrange a filesystem, provide an installer,
configure services, publish updates, and support upgrades. Distributions
made these integration tasks repeatable.

Early projects such as SLS, Slackware, and Debian, followed by commercial
and community distributions including Red Hat and SUSE, offered different
answers. Their importance was not confined to branding. A package database
could record which files belonged to which component. A dependency system
could express that one package needed another. A release policy could
trade newer versions against predictable behavior.

Dependency management does not eliminate compatibility problems.
A package can satisfy a declared version constraint and still expose a
behavioral regression. Locally modified configuration complicates upgrades.
Security fixes may need to be backported without changing a stable
application interface. Distribution engineering therefore involves judgment,
testing, and maintenance over time, not just automated compilation.

The work also creates different notions of ``current.'' A server vendor
may support an older release with carefully selected fixes. A rolling
release may prioritize rapid integration. A developer machine may use
new toolchains while a production machine values stable administration.
These choices are coherent responses to different workloads rather than
evidence that only one distribution has understood Unix correctly.

\section{The network becomes a development medium}

Source availability helped contributors inspect and modify Linux, but
collaboration needed channels and decision processes. Mailing lists,
patches, maintainers, release candidates, and subsystem ownership created
a way to review changes without requiring every developer to understand
every driver. Later, Git became a crucial tool for distributed development.
The development model evolved with the scale of the work.

Large-scale participation did not mean an absence of authority.
Maintainers decide what to accept; reviewers require revisions;
release managers choose when a change is ready. The ability to fork limits
some forms of control but does not make a successful fork cheap.
A project has accumulated tests, reputation, users, expertise, and
relationships that cannot be duplicated by copying a directory alone.

Corporate participation became substantial. Hardware vendors need drivers,
cloud companies need scalability, and distributors need maintainable
releases. Paid work and volunteer work coexist in the same repositories.
The contrast between a purely commercial model and a purely amateur
community is a poor description of the resulting system.

There are genuine tensions. A sponsor's priorities may differ from a
maintainer's, and a widely used subsystem can still depend on too few
reviewers. Public source does not automatically distribute labor fairly.
A history of open source should include the economics of maintenance,
not merely the excitement of the first public release.

\section{Free software and open source name different arguments}

The term ``open source'' gained institutional prominence in 1998.
It provided a way to argue for collaborative development and acceptable
licenses in terms that many businesses found easier to engage with.
The free software movement continued to emphasize users' freedoms as an
ethical objective. The sets of licenses and projects overlap greatly;
the reasons people give for supporting them need not.

Neither movement means software must have a price of zero.
A business can sell support, integration, hosted services, or copies while
complying with relevant license obligations. Conversely, a free download
can remain proprietary. Price, source access, redistribution rights,
and governance should be examined separately.

The distinction becomes important in appliances and services.
A company may publish covered source while making a device difficult to
modify. A network service may use free software internally without giving
users the same practical control over the service. Different licenses
address these situations differently. A broad slogan about ``openness''
cannot replace a description of the actual arrangement.

\section{A new center of gravity}

Linux grew across servers, embedded devices, supercomputing, development
machines, and phones. Its design and implementation changed substantially:
new architectures, multiprocessor scalability, modern filesystems,
security frameworks, virtualization, and extensive device support added
layers far beyond the early 386 kernel. The continuity lies in maintained
interfaces and a development lineage, not in an unchanged small program.

The freely redistributable BSD systems followed their own trajectories.
GNU continued independently across many components. Other kernels and
research systems explored alternatives. The success of Linux did not
collapse these histories into one project, even when they shared tools
and contributors.

A useful lesson is that freedom to combine components enlarges the space
of possible systems. It can produce a conventional command-line server,
a carefully integrated desktop, or a phone that offers no ordinary shell
to its owner. The same legal and technical flexibility can support very
different user experiences.

To understand what endured through that expansion, it helps to stop the
historical clock and examine a smaller specimen. Lions's Sixth Edition
commentary offers an operating system compact enough to follow closely.
Xv6 offers a later teaching reconstruction on different hardware.
Together they reveal which ideas survived and which implementation
assumptions had to be replaced.
